<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arianism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–7th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arianism was a Christian doctrine, named after the Alexandrian priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, holding that the Son was created by the Father and not of the same substance. Condemned at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> in 325, it spread among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Rugians and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, setting their rulers apart from the Nicene Roman population. It faded after the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 589.</p></div>
